{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And YHWH descended upon Mount Sinai.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory said (Sukkah 5a): [He descended] above ten [handbreadths]. Now one needs to know why it was necessary to say <b>to the top of the mountain</b>, since the height [of the descent] was ten [handbreadths] from the mountain, it is obvious that He did not reach even its top. Or perhaps it is because He came to exclude [the possibility] that the Shekhinah descended to the sides of the mountain, and not even to above ten [handbreadths] — although we find that even in low places the Shekhinah descended, for whatever of it reaches the earth is ten handbreadths — go out and learn from the Mishkan: in every place where the children of Israel would camp, the glory of HaShem dwelt there; here, on the mountain, He descended only upon the top of the mountain. And therefore Moses found [it possible] to ascend to the top of the mountain, as He said in the adjacent verse: ״וַיִּקְרָא ה׳ לְמֹשֶׁה אֶל רֹאשׁ הָהָר״." "And one still needs to settle why the verse did not say ״וַיֵּרֶד ה׳ עַל רֹאשׁ הַר סִינַי״. Or perhaps He was concerned about an error in understanding the intent of His words, for in truth He descended upon the totality of Mount Sinai — both its top and its sides — and He said ״upon the top״ because He did not actually rest His Shekhinah upon the mountain in the sense of every single part of the mountain; rather, the place where the Shekhinah of HaShem rested is called ״top,״ by virtue of its being supreme over it, and understand. For this reason He said in this order, <b>And YHWH descended upon Mount Sinai</b> — above ten [handbreadths]; and afterward He went back to inform [us] of the place to which He descended there, which is <b>to the top of the mountain</b>, and understand:" "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to their words of blessed memory, who said (Mekhilta, chapter 9): one verse says ״מִן הַשָּׁמַיִם״ etc. — this teaches that HaShem lowered the supernal heavens etc., and spread them over the mountain and spoke with them — end quote. And this is the meaning of the verse's measure <b>And YHWH descended upon</b> etc. — meaning, He did not literally descend, but rather [remained] above, as alluded to in the word ״upon״; and His heavens, the place of His station, were touching the top of the mountain." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to inform us that when YHWH descended — meaning, when He began to bring down His Shekhinah — the mountain trembled and rose toward Him like a servant running before his master. And this is the meaning of the verse's measure <b>And YHWH descended</b> — and the verse had not yet revealed to which place He descended, and it informed [us] of the mountain's action, despite its being inanimate, that it became animate and rose before He reached it; and afterward the verse revealed to which place He descended, and said <b>to the top of the mountain</b>. From this you learn that the mountain rose before the arrival of the Shekhinah of HaShem upon its top."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:30:23.655439749Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}